From 6acc7f37f0017673458b1b5298cd8fbed41114e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:24:55 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 128/130] render: decode a wallpaper's JPEG / PNG textures on
 other threads while it builds
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A .tex holding a JPEG or PNG (FreeImage format) was decoded by stb_image inside CTexture's constructor, on the GL
thread, one texture at a time. For a scene with large PNG textures that was the largest part of its build: perf
(DWARF call graphs, warm `liascape-scene-worker --lwe-smoke`) put 41% of 3042492564's build in stb's PNG decoder
(inflate 69% of it, unfiltering 25%) and 49% of 3163060610's (56 and 32 PNG textures, 52 and 63 Mpx). zlib's
inflate is no faster than stb's here (2066 of the library's 2067 TEX PNGs decoded both ways: same pixels, same
time), so the decode itself is the real cost; what was wasted is that it ran serially while the rest of the
machine idled.

While Project::render builds the wallpaper, RenderContext now defers the upload of every new JPEG / PNG texture:
CTexture still generates its names and sets its parameters at once, in the same order, but hands each mipmap's
decode to a small pool (at most 4 threads, half the cores below 8; each job owns the mipmap it reads) and uploads
the pixels in finishUpload. Project::render calls finishTextureUploads once the wallpaper is built (and when the
build throws), so the first frame sees every texture; getTextureID finishes a texture first, so nothing can sample
one before its pixels are in, and finishUpload puts back the 2D texture and unpack-buffer bindings it borrows.
Textures created later (scripts' createLayer, the album art) still decode where they are created, as before.
DXT / raw textures are untouched (their uploads keep their place among GL_UNPACK_ALIGNMENT changes); RGBA8 rows
read the same at any alignment.

Measured (warm --lwe-smoke build_ms, the pinned core vs this, alternating, 5 runs each, the NVIDIA shader disk
cache off): 3042492564 2881-2937 ms (median 2903) → 2145-2250 (2186); 3163060610 2258-2407 (2329) → 1815-1862
(1818); 2828233985 814-836 (830) → 720-748 (728); 2371103399 683-703 (695) → 611-659 (622). With the disk cache
on: 3042492564 2058 → 1331 ms, 3163060610 1612 → 1205 (medians). The main thread no longer decodes; the pool is
done well before the build is. What is left of these builds is NVIDIA's GLSL compiler (with no disk cache),
the WE shader text passes and glslang / SPIRV-Cross.

Same output: every toGlsl input was dumped with and without this (the shader sweep: 4085 compiles, 3426 distinct
texts; the 96 WE-ref scenes: 5119 compiles, 586 distinct) — byte-identical, same order per process, and the
sweep's results identical. The WE-ref frames of 3042492564, 3163060610, 2828233985 and 2371103399 are
byte-identical. Of the 96 scenes' 377 frames, 311 are identical; 64 differ between two runs of the old core as
well; 3219908811's clock shows the second before (the build ends sooner under libfaketime); 3413117423's 1 s
frame differs, but its frames differ between any two plain runs of either core and one run of each core gave
byte-identical frames throughout.

Origin: ours
